body:
## Summary

Gzip the request body the admission relay forwards to api.anthropic.com, the same way Claude Code does when it talks to Anthropic directly. If the upstream rejects the encoding, gzip turns off for the process and the original body is resent once.

## Why

Claude Code (2.1.300+, Bun builds) gzips `/v1/messages` bodies of 4096+ characters, but only when the request URL's host is `api.anthropic.com`. The plugin points Claude Code at the loopback relay, so Claude Code sends identity, and the relay forwarded that over the WAN unchanged. A long conversation re-uploads the whole prompt every request. Gzip makes those uploads 2.3–2.8× smaller (measured against the live API; ~11 ms of CPU per MB at level 3).

## What changes (`admission.py`, relay upstream hop only)

- **Gate:** every one of these must hold, mirroring Claude Code's own checks. Otherwise the request is byte-identical to today's.
  - the upstream host is `api.anthropic.com` on the default port;
  - the relay is not on its CONNECT-proxy route;
  - no custom-CA variable is set (`SSL_CERT_FILE`, `SSL_CERT_DIR`, `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE`, `CURL_CA_BUNDLE`, `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`);
  - `CLAUDE_CODE_GZIP_REQUEST_BODIES` is not set to an off value. This is Claude Code's own variable, honored here in the off direction only;
  - the body is ≥ 4096 bytes;
  - gzip has not been latched off;
  - the incoming request has no `Content-Encoding`.
- **Encoding:** one gzip member at level 3 with mtime 0. Before compressing, a newline plus 0–256 random spaces/tabs is appended, as Claude Code does. JSON ignores trailing whitespace.
- **Rejection fallback:** only for a 400/403/415 answer to a gzip send, before any response byte reaches Claude Code. The classifier is Claude Code's, plus two documented upstream answers. A rejection is any of:
  - no `req_` request-id and no `cf-ray`, i.e. a proxy or middlebox refused it;
  - an Anthropic 400 saying the body is not valid JSON, in either of its two messages or Go's `bad json:` form;
  - an Anthropic 415;
  - an Anthropic 400 with `request_body_encoding_unsupported`.
  The "not valid JSON" test is Claude Code 2.1.302's own, read from the binary. A raw body is trimmed at the end only, with JavaScript's `trimEnd` characters. The message prefix counts only inside a full Anthropic error envelope, the `bad_json` string is matched untrimmed, and the anchors behave like JavaScript's `$`. hermes-agent#141666 adds the same classifier to the core Anthropic client; the two agree on a 135-case table.
  On a rejection the relay latches gzip off for the process, logs one warning (reason and status only), and resends the original uncompressed body once on a new connection. Every other answer is forwarded as before, including the bytes it already read.
- **Unchanged:** cancellation still reaches whichever upstream socket is live, and payload transforms (restore, stable context, breakpoint pin) still run first. Prompt caching is unaffected because it works on the decoded request.

Product code: +108/−20 in `admission.py`. About 20 of those lines move the existing connect block into a helper, so the resend can reuse it.

## Live check

- **Setup:** one canary run through Hermes against the real API, Claude Code 2.1.302, per-call, on a 13–16 request coding task. Upstream main and this branch ran back to back.
- **Instrumentation:** a bench-only usage log recorded, per request, whether the body went out gzipped, plus its sizes. No request content was recorded.

| | main | this PR |
|---|---|---|
| Requests | 13 | 16 |
| Sent gzipped | 0 | 16/16 |
| HTTP 200 | 13/13 | 16/16 |
| Bytes on the wire per request | 24–50 KB | 10–22 KB (2.2–2.4× smaller) |
| Cache misses | 0 | 0 |
| Cache-read share of input | 0.90 | 0.94 |
| Latched off | — | never |

Every request read the previous request's cached prefix, so gzip is invisible to prompt caching, as expected.

## Tests

- **New `tests/test_admission_gzip.py` (84 cases), through the real loopback relay with a fake upstream:**
  - every gate case;
  - wire bytes: single member, padding, level 3, mtime 0, exact length and encoding headers;
  - each rejection class both ways, with header-case variants and an empty `cf-ray`;
  - exactly one raw resend, plus the latch;
  - a double rejection;
  - a raw resend that gets no answer, which must not report the rejected answer;
  - no resend on 429/500 or other 400s;
  - a large error body forwarded byte-exact past the pre-read;
  - a stalled error body that times out and still reports its status;
  - streaming 200 unchanged;
  - cancellation between and during attempts.
- **Red-proof:** with compression disabled in the change, 29 of the new cases fail. Mutating the latch, the resend count, the padding, the level, mtime, the origin checks, the prefix forwarding, the proxy, port and kill-switch gates, or the 8192-byte cutoff fails at least one case each.
- **Suite:** 409 passed on macOS. The only failures are the two lifecycle tests that fail on main on macOS. No existing test's expectation changed. The new file also passes on Python 3.13 and 3.14.
- **Offline evals:** `evals/directsdk_admission.py` and `evals/directsdk_cache_wire.py` pass on Claude Code 2.1.302.

## Not proven here

- Windows, beyond CI.
- A rejection by a real middlebox. The classifier and fallback are unit-tested; the live check shows Anthropic accepting the gzip body.
- **Threshold units:** the size floor counts bytes; Claude Code counts JavaScript characters. Mostly non-ASCII bodies of 4–12 KB get gzipped here a little earlier than Claude Code would gzip them. That is harmless.

Stacked on #402 and merges after it (#402 is stacked on #361); only 20 commits are new here, with two merges of #402's head that had no conflict (`74a5ae5`, main `72eb3b8` merged in, then `4b16c5c`); the rest is #402.

Kept mode runs only on the Claude Code releases listed in `plugin.yaml` (`session_versions`). On any other release every call runs per-call until the list names it. This adds one setting for those releases, default unchanged.

## The setting

`session_unlisted_versions`, read from the serving profile's config like the other session settings:

| Value | On a release not on the list |
|---|---|
| `honor` (default) | Every call runs per-call, with one log line. This is today's behaviour and the rule the review of #361 set. |
| `self-test` | The plugin tests that binary itself, offline and in the background. Pass: it keeps processes on it. Fail, timeout, or a test that cannot run: per-call, with the reason logged. |
| `force` | It keeps processes on any release, with one warning. |

Any other value is `honor`. Under `self-test` and `force`, every kept step is still checked before upstream, as on a listed release. A kept process's first request is also held to per-call's own build, as its first resumed request always is. A release whose kept process fails that check runs per-call for the rest of the Hermes process. So a release that frames a request differently costs a restart into per-call, not different bytes.

Each of these is logged once, never per call: the mode on an unlisted release, a self-test's pass, its fail with the reason, and a setting value that is none of the three.

## What a self-test runs

`selftest.py`, in a child Python process, against a loopback upstream that answers the same way every time. It runs one Hermes-shaped conversation twice, per-call and kept:

- several tool rounds, two calls in one step;
- an error result, a whitespace-only result and a large result, each followed by a `/steer`;
- a reply that leads with a whitespace-only block;
- then a compacted history (an empty block, a cache mark on a block that is not the last) and a new turn.

Every kept request must equal per-call's, byte for byte. One process must serve the steps, apart from the expected restarts. The binary must report the version it is tested as. Those are the shapes the review of #402 at `fc71f5d` broke: the test fails against the plugin at `fc71f5d` ("request 4 of 13 differs") and at `74accf4` (no process kept), and passes on every listed release.

| Time per release | |
|---|---|
| Linux, 16 vCPU, each of 2.1.292 to 2.1.326 | 7.8 to 9.9 s |
| macOS, Apple M4, 2.1.292 and 2.1.326 | 4.8 s and 4.3 s |

No call waits for it: until a verdict exists, calls run per-call. One test runs at a time per machine. A failing run is tried once more before the fail is kept. The verdict belongs to that binary's files, the plugin version and the platform, so an update in place, another install or a wrapper is tested anew. It is kept under Hermes' home (`cache/claude-subscription-directsdk-experimental/session-selftest.json`).

**What it is kept away from.** The child gets a throwaway home, Claude config directory and Hermes home, and a placeholder API key. It runs with every plugin setting at its default, so no profile or managed Hermes setting reaches it. On Linux, where unprivileged user namespaces are allowed, it runs in network and pid namespaces of its own: loopback is its only network, and everything in it ends with it. Elsewhere (macOS, Windows, a Linux that refuses namespaces), Claude Code's traffic is pointed at loopback by its settings only; a binary that ignored them could reach the network, holding no login. The child is bounded in time (240 s) and memory, and it ends when Hermes exits or dies. It also ends what it started; without namespaces, that means every process it saw start.

**What a pass proves:** those shapes, on this machine, for this binary. It does not stand in for the list, which names the releases the whole session suite passed on.

## Tests

`tests/test_directsdk_session_unlisted.py`: 36 tests, 60 cases. They cover:

- each mode on a listed and an unlisted release;
- a pass on the real binary, and a fail on a stand-in whose kept requests differ by one byte;
- a binary that reports another version, a timeout, the memory bound;
- a cached verdict, a corrupt cache and a FIFO at the cache path;
- two agents asking at once, another Hermes process testing;
- a binary replaced mid-test;
- Hermes exiting or killed mid-test, with and without namespaces;
- loopback-only inside the namespaces;
- a profile `claude_config_dir` that must not reach the test;
- the mode changed while a process is kept;
- `force` on a release whose kept first request differs.

Each fails with its fix removed: 30 mutations, each caught (logs in the evidence). They also run in `evals/ci.sh`.

| Gate at `6654561` | 2.1.292 | 2.1.322 | 2.1.323 | 2.1.324 | 2.1.325 | 2.1.326 | 2.1.327 | 2.1.328 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Session test files with the new file, Linux | 190 passed | 190 passed | 190 passed | 190 passed | 190 passed | 190 passed | 190 passed | 190 passed |
| macOS 26.6.2 arm64, the new file | 57 passed, 3 skipped | not run | not run | not run | not run | 57 passed, 3 skipped | not run | not run |

Our hosts did not run the full plugin suite or `evals/ci.sh` for this branch at `6654561` (at `b641ed0` they passed: 773, and exit 0 with 133, on 2.1.292 and 2.1.326; #402's head passed both at `74a5ae5`). Upstream CI at `6654561` (run https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-official-plugins/actions/runs/44624965851): the plugin passes on ubuntu-latest (757 passed, 88 skipped; `evals/ci.sh` session tests 130 passed, 3 skipped) and macos-latest (756 passed, 88 skipped; 130 passed, 3 skipped). It fails on windows-latest in `evals/ci.sh`: `test_a_line_the_plugin_does_not_know_restarts_the_kept_process[event-toolu_00_1-event]`, where the bytes differ only in `device_id`. The full suite there passed (728 passed, 116 skipped).

At `514b63d`, the merge of #402's `4b16c5c` (2.1.329 listed; #411's test-harness commit, which writes the device id into the tests' throwaway home up front): on Linux, against hermes-agent main `32dbc1dc`, the session test files with the new file 154 passed on 2.1.329; that is the only run on our hosts at this head. Upstream CI at `514b63d` (run https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-official-plugins/actions/runs/44685885407): the plugin passes on ubuntu-latest (757 passed, 88 skipped; `evals/ci.sh` session tests 130 passed, 3 skipped), macos-latest (756 passed, 88 skipped; 130 passed, 3 skipped) and windows-latest (728 passed, 116 skipped; 114 passed, 19 skipped).

The 3 skipped on macOS are the namespace cases, which need Linux.

## Reviews

- **Grok:** 5 findings, all fixed (cleanup at Hermes exit, temp directories, the shared working directory, two tests).
- **A fresh-context Claude**, twice. First pass: 7 findings and 3 test gaps. Second pass, on the fixes: 3 problems, 3 fixes without a test of their own, and a flaky test. All fixed:
  - the verdict tied to the binary, and the reported version checked;
  - one test per machine, and a failing run tried again;
  - no reused pid's group killed, and temp directories removed;
  - a namespace failure reported with its reason;
  - an unlisted release whose kept launch never matches runs per-call after the first refusal.
- **GPT** (`codex review`), three passes over the range:
  - Hermes exit and an unwritable cache;
  - a binary replaced mid-test, and descendants the child had seen;
  - a probe that raised kept its `max_sessions` place, and a test's watchdog outlived it.
  All fixed.
- **One gpt-6-astra adversarial pass:** 11 findings:
  - a managed Hermes config could reach the child: fixed;
  - the proxy is no network boundary: namespaces on Linux, the limit stated above;
  - a pass on one model enabled unchecked first requests on others: fixed by holding the first kept request to per-call's build;
  - lifecycle and cache items: fixed;
  - a process orphaned between two 250 ms samples, without namespaces: not fixed, stated above.

## Not covered

- Windows: no live run, and none of our hosts is Windows. CI's Windows job passes at `514b63d` (114 passed, 19 skipped in `evals/ci.sh`); the 19 skipped there are not looked into here. At `6654561` it failed on the device id Claude Code makes up in a fresh config directory; #411's commit, now in #402 as `4b16c5c` and merged in here, fixes that in the tests.
- Without namespaces, a process that Claude Code starts and abandons within 250 ms of the child's samples can outlive a killed test.
- macOS: our host's resolver took 35 s to name 127.0.0.1 today, which made the first relay of each process wait 35 s. #412 is on main now and in this head, so the macOS run above has it.
